+# Method-Size Splitting — Layered Design (L1 / L2 / L3)
+
+Status: v1 implementation of L2. Owner: see `git blame` / session handoff.
+
+## Problem
+
+The JVM caps one method's bytecode at 65,535 bytes (`code_length` is a `u2` in the class file — an
+over-limit method cannot even be encoded). Reggie generates specialized matcher classes per pattern,
+so certain patterns (extremely large alternations, big charsets, many-state DFAs) overflow a single
+`matches`/`find`/`match` method.
+
+## Layered strategy (agreed design)
+
+- **L1 — per-generator structural lowering (preferred, done at IR level).** Generators that know
+  their own structure split it into helper methods while emitting (they know local types and state
+  semantically). Precedent: `DFASwitchBytecodeGenerator.STATE_SPLIT_THRESHOLD` (100 states per
+  `$ng_step_N`/`$gt_step_N` bucket helper, ~30 KB each). This remains the preferred layer —
+  generators should lower as far as their structure allows.
+- **L2 — generic in-pipeline splitter (this document, v1).** A safety net for generators that do
+  not implement L1: a `ClassWriter` subclass buffers every emitted method and, if a method is too
+  large, rewrites it as a chain of physical methods. Transparent to generators and to the rest of
+  the class: the first chunk keeps the original method name/descriptor, so all call sites and the
+  structural cache are unaffected.
+- **L3 — JDK fallback (unchanged last resort).** `RuntimeCompiler`'s existing
+  `MethodTooLargeException` catch → `fallbackOrThrow` with diagnostics. Any L2 failure degrades to
+  verbatim emission, which reproduces exactly today's behavior, which lands in this catch.
+
+## L2 architecture: `SplittingClassVisitor`
+
+**Design correction (implementation-time):** ASM 9.10 declares `ClassWriter.visit`/`visitMethod`
+`final` — a `ClassWriter` subclass cannot intercept emission. The pipeline therefore uses a
+wrapping `SplittingClassVisitor extends ClassVisitor` (the standard ASM instrumentation shape):
+both compilation paths construct the real `ClassWriter(COMPUTE_FRAMES | COMPUTE_MAXS)` (still the
+`toByteArray()` producer) and hand generators the wrapping visitor. Since generators' parameters
+were typed `ClassWriter`, they were mechanically widened to `ClassVisitor` (~32 generator files,
+compiler-verified; the only non-visitor use, `RecursiveDescentBytecodeGenerator.generate`'s
+`toByteArray`, was restructured to real-writer + front-visitor). One shared implementation in
+`reggie-codegen`, used by both paths (dual-path rule satisfied at a single change site).
+
+Per method, the writer returns a buffering `MethodVisitor` (ASM tree `MethodNode`):
+
+1. **Buffer** all events (labels are recorded but never bound to any writer; every re-emission
+   uses fresh `Label`s, so multi-writer label aliasing is impossible).
+2. **Size decision** at `visitMaxs` (verified empirically: `MethodTooLargeException` fires at
+   `ClassWriter.toByteArray()`, *not* at `visitMaxs` — the probe must serialize its scratch class):
+   - a *conservative upper-bound sizer* (all jumps sized as wide forms, `ldc_w`, switch padding
+     maxed) says "definitely fits" → replay verbatim into the real writer (zero transformations);
+   - if the conservative size exceeds 65,535, an *exact probe* replays into a scratch `ClassWriter(0)`
+     (fresh labels, fresh pool), calls `toByteArray()`, and catches `MethodTooLargeException` —
+     if the probe fits, replay verbatim;
+   - otherwise run the splitter.
+3. **Splitter** (`MethodSplitter`) — chunks the instruction list; **any failure refuses to split**
+   and replays verbatim (→ real writer throws as today → L3).
+
+### Cut-point invariants (all must hold at an instruction boundary c)
+
+1. **Empty operand stack** at c (per-instruction frames come from ASM's `Analyzer`), so every cut is
+   a statement boundary.
+2. **No jump crosses the cut**, except forward jumps whose target is exactly c (the next chunk's
+   entry) — those become *call + matching return* at the branch site. A crossing edge (u→v):
+   forbidden if `u < c < v` (forward, interior target) or `v < c ≤ u` (backward). This is the v1
+   restriction: **loop back-edges cannot be split across chunks** (a state-machine continuation
+   driver is future work — v2).
+3. **Try/handler atomicity**: the protected region `[start, end)` and the *handler entry* must
+   share a chunk (the exception table is per-method); each `tryCatchBlock` is re-emitted whole into
+   the chunk that contains it. Handler *bodies* may extend across cuts via normal tail chaining —
+   only the entry label is bound to the region's chunk.
+4. **No monitor instructions** anywhere in the method (`monitorenter`/`monitorexit` pair within one
+   method). The compile-time path's `ImplClassBytecodeGenerator` emits synchronized blocks; those
+   methods are simply never split.
+5. **`<init>` / `<clinit>` are never split** (uninitialized-`this` and static-init semantics; also
+   constructor chunking is verifier-hostile). Methods containing them emit verbatim. Big `<clinit>`
+   table init is an L1 concern (e.g., string-encoded table data), not listed for v2.
+6. **Live-slot typing**: local slots live across the cut are passed as extra chunk parameters.
+   Liveness is a standard backward dataflow. Types come from a custom descriptor-based interpreter
+   (`DescriptorInterpreter`): each abstract value is one type descriptor, merges of differing
+   descriptors become UNKNOWN, and a slot that is UNKNOWN at a cut → **the cut is rejected**
+   (never guessed). This replaces ASM's `SourceInterpreter`, whose value-source *sets* grow by one
+   element per loop iteration at merge points (quadratic on loop-carried locals — verified by
+   thread dump) and which attributes locals to their masking `xstore` copies. The extra-parameter
+   count must stay within the JVM's 255-slot limit.
+7. `ACC_SYNCHRONIZED` (flag, not instructions) stays on chunk 0 only — monitors pair per invocation,
+   and inner chunks are plain private methods.
+
+### Chunk shape
+
+- `chunk0` keeps the original access flags, name, descriptor, signature, throws, annotations.
+- `chunkK` (K ≥ 1): `private` (+ `static` iff original static), synthetic, name
+  `<original>$sK`, descriptor = original params + live extra slots → original return type.
+- Local-slot indices are remapped inside each chunk body (params first, then remaining originals);
+  `COMPUTE_MAXS` recomputes `max_locals`/`max_stack`.
+- Tail chaining: chunk's fallthrough tail pushes the next chunk's parameters, invokes it, and
+  returns its result (`xreturn`). Early `return`/`athrow` inside any chunk body is preserved
+  directly — the tail call is in return position, so semantics are exact.
+- Forward jumps into the next chunk's entry are emitted as local-label + call + return blocks.
+- `FrameNode`s are dropped (the real writer recomputes frames); `LineNumberNode`s are kept;
+  `LocalVariableNode` debug tables are dropped in chunks (synthetic).
+
+### Safety-net stack (never worse than status quo)
+
+Everything the splitter cannot guarantee declines to a verbatim replay: the real writer then
+throws `MethodTooLargeException` at `toByteArray()` exactly as today, and callers route it to
+their fallback (L3) or rethrow — the pipeline is never worse than the status quo.
+
+**visitMaxs(0, 0) repair (post-ship fix).** Generators stream into `COMPUTE_MAXS` writers and
+legally declare `visitMaxs(0, 0)` — every real generator does (~230 call sites). The buffered
+`MethodNode` then carries `maxLocals = 0`, and ASM's `Analyzer` sizes its initial `Frame` from
+`node.maxLocals`, refusing at instruction 0 ("inexistant local variable") — so the shipped v1
+silently declined to split *any* real generator method and only synthetic tests (which declared
+real maxima) passed. The fix repairs `node.maxLocals` from the instruction scan and floors
+`node.maxStack` (64) before analysis; both are recomputed by the real writer at serialization,
+so the repair affects the analysis only. A still-too-small repaired capacity surfaces as an
+`AnalyzerException` → decline → verbatim, never corruption.
+
+**Wide-switch methods stay unsplittable in v1.** `buildNoCutZones` runs `forbidCrossing` over
+every switch edge: a dispatch switch whose targets span more than the chunk budget (e.g.
+`NFABytecodeGenerator`'s per-config cascade: a 40k-char literal yields a 4.7 MB
+`findLongestMatchEnd` of ~1.8M instructions dominated by wide switches) marks the whole span
+between the switch and its targets as no-cut → `chooseCuts` starves → decline. Single switch
+instructions wider than 64 KB (40k-label tableswitch ≈ 160 KB) are inherently un-emittable in
+any 64 KB method. That shape is the first confirmed trigger family for v2 driver lowering —
+see "Known v1 exclusions".
+
+conservative sizer → scratch-writer exact probe → per-chunk conservative budget re-validation
+(≈60 KB budget) → real writer's `visitMaxs` guard (throws `MethodTooLargeException` if a margin bug
+survives) → `RuntimeCompiler`'s existing catch → JDK fallback. Splitting can only *improve* outcomes:
+every failure mode replays verbatim and lands exactly where today's code lands.
+
+## Known v1 exclusions / future work
+
+- **v2: backward-edge-crossing cuts** via driver/continuation state machine (chunks return a
+  continuation id; the original method becomes a `tableswitch` driver). Needed for methods whose
+  whole loop body is one straight-line >64 KB span with a single back edge; today those stay on L3.
+  **First confirmed trigger family**: `NFABytecodeGenerator`'s per-config cascade (wide dispatch
+  switches spanning ≫ chunk budget) — a 40k-char literal produces a 4.7 MB `findLongestMatchEnd`
+  that v1 correctly declines (see "Wide-switch methods stay unsplittable in v1").
+- **Wide dispatch switches**: a switch whose targets span more than a chunk needs per-target
+  entry thunks or bucketing within the chunk (or L1 bucketing in the generator, like DFASwitch's
+  `STATE_SPLIT_THRESHOLD`) — v1 declines such methods.
+- **L1 for the NFA generator** (the named offender in `RuntimeCompiler`'s catch comment): NFA state
+  steps as per-state helper methods, like DFASwitch's buckets.
+- Conservative-sizer drift: the sizer over-estimates (wide jumps, max padding); the probe absorbs
+  the gray zone. The only residual inaccuracy is ±ldc width between the scratch pool and the real
+  pool (≤ 1 byte per `ldc`); the real-writer guard covers it.
+
+## Wiring
+
+- `RuntimeCompiler.generateBytecode`: `new ClassWriter(COMPUTE_FRAMES | COMPUTE_MAXS)` →
+  `new SplittingClassWriter(...)`.
+- `ReggieMatcherBytecodeGenerator` (compile-time twin): same swap.
+- No `StructuralHash` changes: the split is invisible externally (chunk 0 keeps the original
+  method header) and no `DFAState`/`DFATransition`/`PatternInfo` fields are added.
+
+## Test status (v1 shipped)
+
+`reggie-codegen: SplittingClassVisitorTest` (all green):
+1. Verbatim path: small method → identical class (no chunks), runs correctly.
+2. Straight-line overflow (~72 KB): splits, loads (JVM verifier validates recomputed frames),
+   computes the exact expected value.
+3. Live ref local (`String`) spanning every cut — exercises chunk-parameter typing.
+4. Forward-crossing if/else chains across cuts → call+return blocks, exact results.
+5. Try/catch region + handler: cuts only before/after; both normal and exceptional paths exact.
+6. Refusal: oversized loop body whose back edge crosses every cut → verbatim →
+   `MethodTooLargeException` propagates (today's behavior preserved).
+7. Post-ship regression: a method declared with `visitMaxs(0, 0)` (what every real generator
+   streams into `COMPUTE_MAXS` writers) still splits — guards the buffered-`MethodNode` maxima
+   repair (found via a 40k-char literal through `NFABytecodeGenerator.findLongestMatchEnd`).
+
+Full regression: `reggie-runtime` 3,152 tests green through the new pipeline; processor and
+integration suites green; `spotlessApply` clean.
+
+**Real-generator note:** probing current pattern families (thousands of distinct-literal capture
+alternations) shows they now route to compact strategies (`BitStateMatcher`, boolean[] lookup
+tables) or hit the compile deadline / determinization limits *before* any method overflows — i.e.
+recent L1 work already absorbed the known overflow families. No pattern in the current test
+corpus is known to overflow a method through the wired-in pipeline, so L2 stands as the safety net
+for unforeseen shapes; the chunked methods are observable via `$s`-suffixed declared methods on the
+generated matcher class when it does fire.
